What I learned about the target from each paper:

PAPER1:
	We're looking at HD189733b, a hot Jupiter, which is likely tidally locked due to it's very close
	orbit to its parent. This first paper focuses on asking if the upper atmosphere can efficently
	distribute heat from the daytime side across into the nightime side, which can be observed
	by looking at the planet's own infrared emission (8µm for this emission) when it is eclipsing
	the star.

	Measuring these data points had challenges since the parent star is active, having spots which
	cause increases of flux up to ±1.5%, 13.4 day rotation period. Measurements were scaled with
	this in mind. 
	There was also 120±4[s] delay when the planet moved behind the star than expected. Multiple
	causes for thought were given such as non-uniform emission of the planet, pertubations by other
	bodies, but the most likely cause was eccentricity of the planet's orbit (as well as time
	delay for light to cross the system). Min and max temp for the planet's emission was
	973±33[K] and 1212±11[K].

PAPER2:
	since then, Redfield has detected atomic sodium in it's atmosphere. Using infrared gives
	a way better planet / star contrast ratio to better differentiate between the two as well.
	(no way i see dr. harrington here in a citation) Planet to Star surface area ratio is 2nd
	largest of all known transiting exoplanets (at least in 2008 of course)
	This one will go through to get values for planet and stellar R, inclination i, transit time
	T_c, and orbital period P. There's also a companion M star! 
	
	Analysis has to be split between two detectors due to differeing chemistry with both of them,
	which is noted in the paper. (InSb, Si:As, which correspond to the (3.6, 4.5 µm) and 
	(5.8, 8.0µm) channels. The effect mentioned in class and in the other paper about the effective
	gain of the pixels increasing with time is here too, "detector ramp". Within MIPS observation,
	they talk about the 3\sig clipping from the median value too, that's cool.
	Water absorption within the spectra too. (contrast to another planet that has emission)
	Some comparisons to that planet are noted. 

	Observed data from 3.6 - 8.0µm favors a model that assumes no redist of energy to the night
	side, but in constrast with the previous paper's findings with a relatively flat min/max temp.
	Could be that the planet emits much less at shorter wavelengths, and also distribution might
	be depth dependent in the atmosphere.

	In this paper, 5 / 6 bandpasses say that the eclipise center occurs at time expected of a
	circular orbit, and not that of an offset like before. However eclipse at 3.6µm observed
	to arrive 5.6±0.8 minutes later than expected. Def not orbital variations, but maybe
	from brightness variations across visible hemisphere of planet. But, this band is the one 
	with the largest corrections from the time dep. sensitivity, and largest noise.

